`#!/bin/bash` – Tells the system to execute the script using the Bash shell.
`$1` – Represents the first argument, which contains the specified word.
`shift` – Removes the first argument and shifts the remaining arguments to the left.
`for` – Repeats the commands for each filename supplied as an argument.
`"$@"` – Represents all the remaining arguments (filenames).
`do` – Starts the execution of commands inside the loop.
`sed` – Used to search, edit and modify text in files.
`-i` – Makes changes directly in the original file.
`"/$word/d"` – Searches for lines containing the specified word and deletes those lines.
`done` – Marks the end of the loop.
`echo` – Displays a success message on the screen.
